Write the brief for: $ARGUMENTS

You write the brief; you never run the work it describes. A brief is one file,
`.claude/briefs/<slug>.md` — the slug is ASCII `a-z`, `0-9` and hyphens, in English, at most 80
characters, saying what it is about (`billing-retry`) — written with the **Write tool** after every
answer, so nothing is lost when I close the session, and shown as a page in my browser. The target
skill reads it instead of interviewing me again. These hold throughout:
- Ask **one question at a time**, with 2–4 options, the **recommended one first** with a one-line
  reason; I can always type my own answer. Never label an option with the bare word `brief`: typed
  alone, it opens the page instead of reaching you.
- **Never ask what the code already answers**: look first, then ask. Say what you inferred.
- Name what the brief refers back to — criteria, risks, workstreams — by kebab-case slugs, never
  by codes (the project rule *Naming what you refer back to*).
- Before every step, **Read the brief again**: I may have edited it in the page, and the file wins
  over your memory of it.
- Keep the headings and the `Key:` lines exactly as in the template below: ocgen parses them for
  the page{% if not english_prompts %}. Write the text itself in {{ prompt_language }} unless I ask for another
  language{% endif %}.

## 0. Resume, or start

First, where you are: `git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-dir --git-common-dir`. Two
different paths mean a worktree. A brief written here goes when the worktree does, and the main
checkout never sees it{% if workstreams %}; `/workstreams` runs only in the main checkout, so never write a
`workstreams` brief here — tell me to run `/brief` there. For any other target, warn{% else %}. Warn{% endif %} me before
writing anything and go on only when the brief is for this worktree's own work (a worker's), or
when I say so. Then make sure `.claude/briefs/.gitignore` exists and holds the single line `*`
(the folder keeps itself, and the briefs, out of git).

- **No arguments:** list the briefs in `.claude/briefs/` — slug, `Target:`, `Status:`, `Updated:`
  — newest first, and let me pick one to continue; with none, ask for the goal.
- **With arguments:** match an existing brief by its slug or its Goal and continue it from where
  its `Status:` and its Open section say it stands. Otherwise the arguments are the goal of a
  new brief.

The page opens by itself after every write: a hook shows the brief in an editor, with a coverage
strip (one cell per section the target needs: missing, thin, covered), what the dry run still asks,
the target, and Copy buttons; what I save there is in the file the next time you Read it. When you
resume a brief, refresh its `Updated:` and `Commit:` lines and write the file: that puts its page in
front of me again. If no page opens (no ocgen on PATH, or a browser switched off), carry on — the
file is the record, and `brief` typed alone opens the page at any time.

## 1. Route

Classify the goal, say the classification in one line, and let me override it:
{% if deliver %}- **deliver** — build or change something → the brief for `/deliver`.
{% endif %}{% if inquire %}- **inquire** — understand how or why the code works, no change wanted yet → the brief for
  `/inquire` (see *When the target is /inquire* below).
{% endif %}{% if workstreams %}- **workstreams** — several independent pieces, each needing its own commits and checks → the
  brief for `/workstreams`; one piece is {% if deliver %}`deliver`{% else %}not a split{% endif %}.
{% endif %}The target decides which sections the brief needs (the template below). Write the header
and the Goal now, with `Status: draft`.

## 2. Skim

Read-only, in parallel ({{ explorer }} subagents where useful): the entry points, files and types
the goal touches, what is already true, the tests and the check command, recent git activity on
those files. Put what you found under **Context** as `file:line` facts: this is what makes every
question informed and keeps you from asking what the code answers.{% if workstreams %} For **workstreams**, check
before the interview that the pieces you would propose touch disjoint files (Grep the paths,
`git log --oneline -- <path>` for recent churn): two pieces that edit the same file are one
workstream.{% endif %}

## 3. Interview

One question at a time until every section the target needs is covered: scope, success criteria
(one per line, each with its slug and how to see it), constraints, out of scope, risks{% if workstreams %}; for
workstreams, the split itself — slug, files, `After:`, worker kind (**session** for anything with
commits or a check the worker runs{% if deliver %}, or the `/deliver` pipeline{% endif %}; **subagent**{% if implementer %} — `{{ implementer }}` —{% endif %} only for
one edit set with no commit and no check run by the worker), check and criteria{% endif %}.{% if scope_guard %} `{{ scope_guard }}`
checks a change against the criteria's slugs and the Scope line, so write them to be checked.{% endif %}
After **each** answer rewrite the whole file with the Write tool — not Edit, not Bash: the write is
what refreshes the page. What you fill in without asking goes under **Inferred** as
`- <slug>: <what you assumed, and from what>`; I contest an item by naming its slug, and a
contested item becomes a question. Skip a question whose answer is already in the brief or the
code.

## 4. Dry run

When the sections are covered, ask the target itself what it would still ask. Start a **fresh,
read-only subagent** ({{ explorer }}; it gets no conversation context) with exactly this: the path
of the target's skill, `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/../<target>/SKILL.md` (where this skill was loaded
from, so it is there in a worktree and in a plugin too), the path of the brief, and one question —
*"Read both; if you cannot read the skill, answer `cannot read the skill` and nothing else. Given
this brief and the code it cites (read only the cited `file:line` ranges), which questions would
this skill still have to ask the user in its first phases ({{ dry_run_phases }}) before it could
present its plan, board or first answer? One line each: the question, the phase it comes from, why
the brief doesn't settle it. Write `none` when nothing is left."* It returns that list and nothing
else. Then take each returned question in turn:
- answered in the brief already → drop it, and tighten the wording that hid the answer;
- answerable from the code → answer it yourself: the fact goes under Context, your conclusion
  under Inferred;
- otherwise → ask me, one question at a time, and write after each answer.
Run the dry run again on the updated brief — **at most 3 rounds**, or until it returns `none`.
What is still open after that stays under **Open**, one line each: the target asks those, you
don't.

## 5. Polish

Rewrite the prose with the `/improve-prompt` technique — the role the brief speaks to, the task,
success criteria with their slugs, constraints, context, an example only where it removes a doubt
— without adding, removing or renaming a section or a key, and without changing what any answer
means{% if inquire %}; **What I know** and **First question** stay word for word, they are mine{% endif %}. Tell me in
one line what changed in meaning, or `wording only`; the page shows the text.

## 6. Hand off

Set `Status: ready`, refresh `Updated:` and `Commit:`, write the file, and print the `Hand-off:`
line as the one thing to type next. Then **stop**: never start the target yourself, not even when
asked in the same message — say the command and wait. For a worker session in a worktree, which
cannot read this checkout, the page's **Copy brief** button gives the whole text to paste as that
session's first message.

## The brief

Every brief has the header, **Open** and **Inferred**; the other sections are the ones marked for
its target. Keep the keys and headings in English.

````markdown
# Brief: <slug>
Target: {{ targets }}
Status: draft | ready
Updated: <date> · Commit: <git rev-parse --short HEAD>
Hand-off: /<target> .claude/briefs/<slug>.md

## Goal
<what and why, two to five sentences{% if inquire %}; inquire: the topic, and why I study it{% endif %}>

## Context
<what the skim found: entry points, files, file:line facts, what is already true>

## Scope
<what changes and what doesn't; the refactors, renames or dependency changes the goal needs{% if inquire %} (not for inquire){% endif %}>

## Success criteria
- <criterion-slug>: <what must be true, and how to see it{% if inquire %}; inquire: what I want to be able to explain{% endif %}>

## Constraints
<must and must-not{% if inquire %}; inquire: the boundaries of the topic, and the lenses to favour{% endif %}>

## Out of scope
<what not to do>

## Risks
- <risk-slug>: <what could go wrong, and how you would see it{% if inquire %} (not for inquire){% endif %}>
{% if workstreams %}
## Workstreams
- Workstream: <slug> — Files: <paths> — After: none | <slug> — Worker: session | subagent — Check: <command{% if check_cmd %}; `{{ check_cmd }}` unless this piece needs another{% endif %}> — Criteria: <criterion-slug>, <criterion-slug>
{% endif %}{% if inquire %}
## What I know
- assumed | verified: <inquire only: one bullet of my mental model, verified ones with their file:line>

## First question
<inquire only: my own first question, sharpened with me>
{% endif %}
## Open
- <what the dry run still asks; empty when ready>

## Inferred
- <slug>: <what you filled in without asking, and from what>
````
{% if inquire %}
## When the target is /inquire

`/inquire` teaches me to ask by making me form each question myself — its hints point, they never
ask — so the brief must not do it for me: write no list of questions, and **never write the next
question for me**. The brief carries the topic and why I study it (Goal); what I already know or
assume (What I know, each bullet `assumed` until something verifies it; the skim may mark one
`verified` with its `file:line`); the boundaries and the lenses to favour — structure, flow,
contract, rationale, change impact, failure (Constraints); and my own **First question**,
sharpened with me the way `/inquire` does it: if it is vague or rests on an assumption, offer 2–3
sharper variants, recommended first, and I pick. The dry run for `/inquire` asks only what orient
and sharpen would still need, never what I should ask next; Open holds those needs, never a
question for me to study.
{% endif %}
